  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Sikorsky Aircraft obligated $2.9B in federal contract dollars in FY2026 to date. Sikorsky recorded 11,960 awards with an average award size of $242K, and obligations fell 40.1% year over year. Historical obligations show variability: FY21 $4.9B, FY22 $3.4B, FY23 $5.3B, FY24 $4.8B, FY25 $4.8B, and FY26 $2.9B. The Department of Defense is by far the largest buyer, obligating $2.8B. Other buyers in FY2026 to date include the Department of Homeland Security with $74.8M and the Department of Justice with $8.2M. Aircraft Manufacturing is the dominant market, accounting for $2.5B of obligations. Secondary markets include Other Aircraft Parts and Auxiliary Equipment Manufacturing at $313M and Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing at $21M. Smaller NAICS markets recorded obligations ranging from $19.9M to $167K. These figures are current fiscal year to date and represent contract obligations except where noted as total contract values.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are Sikorsky Aircraft's federal obligations?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the primary buyer, obligating $2.8B in FY2026 to date. Total obligations of $2.9B show a heavy reliance on one agency. The next-largest buyers are the Department of Homeland Security at $74.8M and the Department of Justice at $8.2M, which together represent a small share of the total."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries account for most of Sikorsky Aircraft's federal revenue?",
        "body": "Aircraft Manufacturing accounts for $2.5B of obligations and is the largest market. Other Aircraft Parts and Auxiliary Equipment Manufacturing accounts for $313M, followed by Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing at $21M. Remaining markets range from $19.9M down to $167K, indicating a primary focus on aircraft and related parts."
      }
    ]
  },
